{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong level bias (mostly under-forecast): in sustained uptrends the model sits ~5–12 EUR/t below realized prices; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the last 5–10 days show a clear positive slope, the forecast fails to propagate the momentum; add a trend term proportional to recent slope (e.g., 5–10d linear trend) and let it compound over the horizon.",
    "Horizon-specific error structure: near-term (days 1–5) errors are modest; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) errors expand materially. Use a horizon ramp correction rather than a constant offset.",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp rises, the model mean-reverts too early and too strongly (predicts pullbacks that don’t materialize immediately). Reduce mean-reversion strength for ~1–2 weeks following a breakout/spike.",
    "Volatility damping: forecast path is too smooth versus realized moves; increase amplitude (widen excursions) when recent realized volatility is elevated, especially during regime shifts.",
    "Regime-switch heuristic: if price breaks above the recent 20–30d range/high, treat it as a new higher regime (re-anchor forecasts to the new level) instead of reverting toward the prior range."
  ]
}
